# Who has to review what.
#
# Every gate in this repository has the same defect waiting for it: the code that judges a pull
# request must not be changeable by that pull request. Branch protection turns these entries into a
# requirement; on their own they are routing. The settings that make them bite are in
# docs/BRANCH-PROTECTION.md.
#
# The boundary, in three parts:
#
#  1. **The checker.** The policy engine, the scanner wrapper, the CLI and the CI scripts decide
#     what a verdict is. A pull request that edits them is editing its own judge, so they need a
#     review from the owner of the gate — and the quality gate goes further and runs an extracted
#     copy of `src/` and `scripts/` from the base branch anyway.
#  2. **The reference line.** Approved baselines and accessibility snapshots are the trust anchor a
#     comparison is made against. Changing one is changing what the gate accepts, so it is reviewed
#     like code, never as an artifact refresh.
#  3. **The dependencies.** The gates run in a pinned container, but the installed packages come from
#     the lockfile in the pull request. A dependency change is inside the trust boundary until the
#     checker gets its own locked install; these files are how that change gets eyes on it.

# The default owner for anything not listed below.
*                                   @Azmk1

# The checker: what a verdict is made of.
/src/                               @Azmk1
/scripts/                           @Azmk1
/package.json                       @Azmk1
/pnpm-lock.yaml                     @Azmk1
/tsconfig.json                      @Azmk1
/next.config.ts                     @Azmk1

# The gates themselves, their workflows and the registered routes they audit.
/.github/                           @Azmk1
/examples/ci/                       @Azmk1

# The reference line: an approval is a reviewed decision, not a side effect.
/examples/uiproof-baselines/        @Azmk1

# The application under test. It is expected to change in pull requests — that is the point of the
# gate — but a change here is what the walkthroughs reason about, so it is reviewed too.
/examples/example-app/              @Azmk1
